## Quick‑Start Guide  
Below you’ll find a step‑by‑step checklist that lets **you** (the site owner) add a secure “Admin” page that shows every customer’s orders.  
Everything relies on Supabase’s built‑in authentication and row‑level security (RLS). No need to write any server‑side code unless you want extra polish.

> **Prerequisites**  
> *   You already have a Supabase project with an **orders** table.  
> *   The orders table has at least these columns: `id`, `user_id` (the customer who placed it), `product`, `amount`, `created_at`.  
> *   You’re using the official Supabase JavaScript SDK in a React, Vue, Next.js, Svelte, or plain HTML/JS app.  
> *   You know how to log in users with Supabase Auth and how to fetch data with `supabase.from('orders').select(...)`.

> **Goal**  
> 1️⃣ Create a **super‑user** (or “admin”) flag on the Auth provider.  
> 2️⃣ Add RLS policies so only that super‑user can read all rows.  
> 3️⃣ Render a simple admin page that fetches and shows the orders list.

---

## 1️⃣ Mark Your Email as the Admin

1. In the Supabase dashboard, go to **Authentication → Providers → Email**.  
2. Open **"Email Sign‑ups"** or **"Sign‑ups & invites"** section.  
3. Add your own email (**e.g.** `you@example.com`) to the **"Admins list"** and click **Save**.  
   - This marks that account as a Super‑Admin.

> **Tip**: Supabase automatically assigns that account a special `role: "admin"` claim in the JWT.

---

## 2️⃣ Ensure Your Database Uses Row‑Level Security (RLS)

1. Open **Table Editor** → **orders**.  
2. Turn on **Row Level Security** (checkbox on the right).  

> **Why?** By default, Supabase lets all authenticated users see everything once RLS is on. We’ll override that with policies.

---

## 3️⃣ Create Two Policies

### a) Policy for Public Users (Customers)

```sql
-- Allow customers to *only* see their own orders
CREATE POLICY "Customers can view own orders"
ON orders
FOR SELECT
USING (
  auth.uid() = user_id
);
```

### b) Policy for the Admin

```sql
-- Allow the admin to view *all* orders
CREATE POLICY "Admin can view all orders"
ON orders
FOR SELECT
USING (
  auth.role() = 'admin'          -- claim set in Supabase auth
  OR auth.uid() = 'YOUR_SUPABASE_ADMIN_UID'   -- fallback, optional
);
```

> **Explanation**  
> * `auth.role()` is automatically set for users in the “Admins list.”  
> * `auth.uid()` is the unique ID of every authenticated user.  
> * The `OR` clause is optional; it lets you specify a specific UID if you prefer.

> **Saving Policies**  
> After each `CREATE POLICY` run, click the “Save Changes” button (or simply commit the DDL).

---

## 4️⃣ (Optional) Add a “roles” Column for Extra Flexibility

If you ever want more granular roles (e.g., `manager`, `support`), add a column:

```sql
ALTER TABLE orders
ADD COLUMN role text DEFAULT 'customer';
```

Then adjust the policies accordingly.  
However, for a single‑admin use‑case, the `auth.role()` claim is sufficient.

---

## 5️⃣ Front‑End: Build the Admin Page

Below is a minimal React example. Replace the placeholders with your own style.

```tsx
// AdminDashboard.tsx
import { createClient } from '@supabase/supabase-js';
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

const supabaseUrl = import.meta.env.VITE_SUPABASE_URL;   // PROD
const supabaseAnonKey = import.meta.env.VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY;
const supabase = createClient(supabaseUrl, supabaseAnonKey);

export const AdminDashboard = () => {
  const [orders, setOrders] = useState<any[]>([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const [user, setUser] = useState<any>(null);

  // 1️⃣ Check if we’re logged in
  useEffect(() => {
    const session = supabase.auth.getSession();
    setUser(session.user);
  }, []);

  // 2️⃣ Fetch all orders
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!user) return;  // not logged in

    const fetchOrders = async () => {
      const { data, error } = await supabase
        .from('orders')
        .select('*')
        .order('created_at', { ascending: false });

      if (error) {
        setError(error.message);
      } else {
        setOrders(data ?? []);
      }
      setLoading(false);
    };

    fetchOrders();
  }, [user]);

  // 3️⃣ Render
  if (loading) return <p>Loading…</p>;
  if (error) return <p style={{ color: 'red' }}>{error}</p>;
  if (!user || user.role !== 'admin') return <p>This page is for admins only.</p>;

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>All Customer Orders</h1>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>#</th>
            <th>Customer</th>
            <th>Product</th>
            <th>Amount</th>
            <th>Date</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {orders.map((o: any) => (
            <tr key={o.id}>
              <td>{o.id}</td>
              <td>{o.user_id}</td>
              <td>{o.product}</td>
              <td>{o.amount}</td>
              <td>{new Date(o.created_at).toLocaleString()}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  );
};
```

### Where to Put It

- **If you’re using React/Vite**: Add a route `/admin` that renders `AdminDashboard`.  
- **If you’re using Next.js**: Put this in `pages/admin.tsx` and wrap it with `requireAuth`.
- **If you’re on plain HTML/JS**: Include the script in a page only accessible after login.

---

